  <h2>Why grants alone are risky</h2>
  <p>
    Grants matter, but they’re competitive, slow, and often restricted to a specific program — which makes
    them a shaky foundation for payroll, rent, or year-round services. The fix isn’t to abandon grants; it’s
    to pair them with <strong>revenue that recurs on its own</strong> so your budget doesn’t swing with a
    single funding decision.
  </p>

  <h2>Recurring and passive options that don’t need a grant</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Monthly giving</strong> — a sustaining-donor program turns one-time gifts into predictable income.</li>
    <li><strong>Employer matching</strong> — many employers double a supporter’s gift for free; most matches go unclaimed.</li>
    <li><strong>Shopping portals & gift cards</strong> — a share of supporters’ everyday online purchases is passed to your cause.</li>
    <li><strong>Local profit-share</strong> — a slice of local business profit funds your organization every time neighbors shop.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    For the full honest comparison — including realistic yields and the trade-offs of each — see
    <a href="/learn/passive-nonprofit-funding/">passive nonprofit funding: every honest option</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>How the Good Circles local profit-share works</h2>
  <p>
    Good Circles ties nonprofit funding to local commerce. On every <strong>$100</strong> a neighbor spends at
    a participating local business, about <strong>{dollars(PER_100.nonprofit)}</strong> — {nonprofitPct}% of
    the merchant’s profit — goes to a nonprofit that neighbors elected, at no extra cost to the shopper (the
    shopper actually saves about {dollars(PER_100.shopperSaves)}). Because it’s tied to routine spending, the
    revenue recurs month after month rather than arriving as a one-off.
  </p>
  <p>
    Participation is free, and the causes are chosen by the community, not by the platform. Learn more on our
    <a href="/for-nonprofits/">nonprofits page</a>, or see the broader Mississippi picture on
    <a href="/mississippi-nonprofit-fundraising/">Mississippi nonprofit fundraising</a>.
  </p>
